BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:now and then Artist/Curator Talk: Michael Massie in Conversation w
 ith Alysa Procida
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261028T183000
UID:curator-talk-michael-massie-conversation-alysa-procida@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Don't miss this exclusive Artist/Curator Talk with acclaimed a
 rtist Michael Massie and guest curator Alysa Procida.\n\nnow and then Arti
 st/Curator Talk: Michael Massie in Conversation with Alysa Procida Wednesd
 ay\, October 28\, 5 p.m. to 6:30 p.m. Don't miss this exclusive Artist/Cur
 ator Talk with acclaimed artist Michael Massie and guest curator Alysa Pro
 cida . Discover the stories exhibited in now and then \, celebrating Massi
 e's remarkable career through an extraordinary collection of teapots\, pre
 viously unexhibited and new works. Together\, they will explore the joy\, 
 humour\, and experimentation that define Massie's practice\, and discuss h
 ow his exceptional craftsmanship weaves together Inuit design traditions\,
  community\, and political commentary while continually challenging expect
 ations of contemporary Inuit art. Visit Registration Page About the Artist
  Michael Massie was born in Happy Valley-Goose Bay and currently resides i
 n Kippens\, NL. His artwork reflects his mixed Inuit and Scottish heritage
 \, such as his iconic ulu teapot works. He has been working in sculpture\,
  painting\, and drawing for over thirty years and has achieved artistic ac
 claim in Canada and internationally. His work sits in collections includin
 g the Smithsonian National Museum of the American Indian\, Heard Museum\, 
 Royal Ontario Museum\, National Gallery of Canada\, The Rooms\, Winnipeg A
 rt Gallery\, Montreal Museum of Fine Art\, and the YVR Vancouver Airport C
 ollection. He has been shortlisted twice for the Prix Saidye-Bronfman\, an
  honorary distinction for a Canadian craftsperson who demonstrates excelle
 nce in fine crafts. He was elected a member of the Royal Canadian Academy 
 of Arts in 2011 and inducted into the Order of Canada in 2017. Massie rece
 ived an honorary doctorate from OCAD University in 2024. About the Guest C
 urator Alysa Procida is an arts administrator\, curator\, and consultant w
 ho is passionate about connecting artists with audiences across the world.
  She is the 2026 International Liaison for the Arctic Arts Summit Secretar
 iat\, having previously co-organized the 2022 Arctic Arts Summit in Whiteh
 orse\, YK. Previously\, she was the Executive Director of the Inuit Art Fo
 undation (IAF) and Publisher of the Inuit Art Quarterly (IAQ)\, Chair of t
 he Magazines Canada Board of Directors\, and a mentor on the Inuit Futures
  in Arts Leadership: The Pilimmaksarniq / Pijariuqsarniq Project. As Publi
 sher of the IAQ \, Alysa oversaw a full redesign of the magazine in conjun
 ction with the IAF’s 30th anniversary in 2017 and the magazine’s first
  National Magazine Award in 2018. In 2019\, she was shortlisted for Publis
 her Grand Prix at the National Magazine Awards\, and in 2021 the IAQ was a
 warded Best Arts and Literary Magazine and winner of the Magazine Grand Pr
 ix. Onsite Gallery is generously supported by The Delaney Family We also a
 cknowledge the support of Ontario Arts Council\, an agency of the Ontario 
 Government and City of Toronto through Toronto Arts Council.
LOCATION:Onsite Gallery (RHD) 199 Richmond Street West 199 Richmond Street
  West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/now-and-then-artist/curato
 r-talk-michael-massie-conversation-alysa-procida
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wellness Week!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T235959
UID:wellness-week@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Free food\, raffles/prizes\, workshops and more!\n\nA 3-day Ev
 ent: October 27-29\n\nFollow us on Instagram to keep up with updates!\n\nT
 he Student Wellness Centre will be hosting a 3-day wellness event this yea
 r. There will be food\, activities\, prizes and an opportunity to learn ab
 out the different resources available to OCAD U students.\n\nCheck out wha
 t we will be offering each day below. We hope to see you there!\n\nWellnes
 s Tabling Event October 27th 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. 100 McCaul St.\, Main Lobby
  We will be hosting a tabling event providing students with academic\, men
 tal/physical health resources! There will be free hot lunch\, activities a
 nd a chance to enter our raffle for giveaways such as gift cards and Saje 
 wellness bundles. Who you can expect! Student Wellbeing Centre for Learnin
 g & Teaching International Student's Office Alumo Good2Talk Campus Life We
 st Toronto Ontario Health Team\n\nHarm Reduction Workshop October 28th 12 
 p.m. 230 Richmond St W.\, Student Living Room (8F) The SWC within OCAD U h
 as partnered with local community health centres\, other colleges and univ
 ersities to begin connecting with students about Harm Reduction. Come join
  us for a free pizza lunch and meet some of our partners who are doing ama
 zing Harm Reduction work in Toronto. The workshop will allow for students 
 to learn and ask questions about Harm Reduction (ie: safer drug use practi
 ces\, the toxic toronto drug supply\, nalaxone\, drug prohibition/mass inc
 arceration\, HIV and Hep C) and to provide input on how the wellness centr
 e can implement this program effectively. Workshop led by Chance Cordon\, 
 Manager\, Harm Reduction Project.\n\nDecompression Day October 29th 10 a.m
 . to 6 p.m. 230 Richmond St W.\, Student Living Room (8F) As important as 
 it is to be proactive with your wellness\, sometimes its about decompressi
 ng and winding down. Come by the Student Living Room to enjoy a relaxing a
 ctivity day. There will be paint-your-own mugs (while supplies last)\, sna
 cks and vibey music.
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street & Student Living Room
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/wellness-week
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Drawing Out: Art Now
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T235959
UID:drawing-out-art-now@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:A three-day Art festival curated by James Olley and Amy Swartz
  marking OCAD University's 150-year legacy through performance\, participa
 tion\, and expanded approaches to drawing.\n\nDrawing Out: Art Now Drawing
  Out: Art Now is a three-day Art festival curated by James Olley and Amy S
 wartz marking OCAD University's 150-year legacy through performance\, part
 icipation\, and expanded approaches to drawing. The program foregrounds co
 ntemporary art practices in which gesture\, movement\, and live action int
 ersect with mark-making. In this context\, drawing is an act of movement: 
 a way of thinking\, understanding\, and making through the body\, becoming
  both process and trace. Tuesday\, October 27 to Thursday\, October 29\, 2
 026 OCAD University\, The Great Hall\n\nSchedule Tuesday\, October 27 5:30
  PM Door Open 6:00 - 9:00 PM Drawing Disco - Draw\, dance and socialize Er
 in Finley Hors d'oeuvres\, Refreshments Above Ground Drawing Supplies incl
 uded +2 Raffle Door Prizes worth $250 each! Ticket: Wednesday\, October 28
  1:30 - 4:30 PM Drawing in 4 Parts: Folding\, Transformation\, and Process
  Natalie Waldburger | Cybèle Young | Sophie Dunkley | TooPink Free event 
 Thursday\, October 29 4:30 Door Open 5:00 - 8:00 PM Drawing to Close: Gest
 ure\, Performance\, and Transformation Francisco-Fernando Granados | James
  Knott | Pixel Heller RSVP here:
LOCATION:The Great Hall 100 McCaul St.
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/drawing-out-art-now
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:In Conversation | Rebecca Baird and Kenny Baird with Lisa Deanne S
 mith and Dr. Lilian Radovac
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T150000
UID:conversation-rebecca-baird-and-kenny-baird-lisa-deanne-smith-and-dr-li
 lian@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an engaging conversation and exhibition tour with 
 artists Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvin Baird\, alongside exhibition curator
  Lisa Deanne Smith and scholar Dr. Lilian Radovac\, as they explore the st
 ories\, creative processes\, and lived experiences behind Returning to Our
 selves.\n\nIn Conversation | Rebecca Baird and Kenny Baird with Lisa Deann
 e Smith and Dr. Lilian Radovac – Saturday\, October 24\, 2026\, 1:00 p.m
 . to 3:00 p.m.\, Onsite Gallery\, 199 Richmond Street West\, Toronto Join 
 us for an engaging conversation and exhibition tour with artists Rebecca B
 aird and Kenny Alvin Baird \, alongside exhibition curator Lisa Deanne Smi
 th and scholar Dr. Lilian Radovac \, as they explore the stories\, creativ
 e processes\, and lived experiences behind Returning to Ourselves. Spannin
 g more than four decades of artistic practice\, Returning to Ourselves sho
 wcases the transformative work of Rebecca and Kenny Alvin Baird through sc
 ulpture\, painting\, traditional material practices\, interior design\, fi
 lm art direction\, graphic design\, and public art commissions. Together\,
  the artists and curator will reflect on the exhibition's themes of identi
 ty\, family\, creativity\, and Cree Métis cultural continuity\, offering 
 unique insights into the evolution of their multidisciplinary practices an
 d the enduring impact of their work. Visit Registration Page Image banner 
 credit: Rebecca Baird and Kenny Alvin Baird \, 1991. Image courtesy of the
  artists.
LOCATION:Onsite Gallery (RHD) 199 Richmond Street West 199 Richmond Street
  West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/conversation-rebecca-baird
 -and-kenny-baird-lisa-deanne-smith-and-dr-lilian
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Creates Change Richard Fung
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T190057
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T210057
UID:art-creates-change-richard-fung@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:OCAD University’s Faculty of Art presents: ART CREATES CHANG
 E The Kim Pruesse Speakers Series\n\nThe Kim Pruesse Speakers Series Richa
 rd Fung Artist Talk & Reception: Thursday\, October 22\, 7 pm OCAD Univers
 ity Auditorium 100 McCaul St.\, M5T 1W1\, Level 1\, Room 190 Free to the p
 ublic Wheelchair accessible Art Creates Change is a long-running lecture s
 eries\, now in its 20th year\, sponsored by the Faculty of Art at OCAD Uni
 versity. The series focuses on artists whose work engages and challenges t
 he conventions of creative practice and political imperatives\, and their 
 various locations of agency. Events are often co-sponsored with programs a
 nd organizations both within and beyond the university. This year\, in rec
 ognition of OCAD University’s 150th anniversary\, we are focusing on thr
 ee faculty members: Bonnie Devine\, Richard Fung and Lillian Allen\, three
  artists whose work has helped shape not only OCAD\, but also the larger c
 ulture and political conversations of our time. Their creative\, academic 
 and political work organically engages urgent public conversations about r
 epresentation\, justice\, equity and relational power\, and embodies OCAD
 ’s vision of “a vibrant community of bold\, curious and compassionate 
 artists\, designers and scholars imagining a creative\, joyful\, equitable
  and sustainable world.” Events are often co-sponsored with programs and
  organizations both within and beyond the university. Each event includes 
 an artist presentation\, Q&A\, and a following reception. The events are f
 ree and open to the public and university communities.\n\nThe Art Creates 
 Change 2026 is supported by Mark S Bonham Centre for Sexual Diversity Stud
 ies at UT OCAD U Art & Social Change INTM: LIFE STUDIES SPECIALIZATION in 
 the Faculty of Art through a generous donation of the Joan and Clifford Ha
 tch Foundation. Follow OCAD U Faculty of Art on Social Media 2026 Co-progr
 ammers: b.h. Yael & Min Sook Lee\n\nAbout Richard Fung Richard Fung’s br
 oad range of work\, from his acclaimed videos to his critical writing\, pr
 ovide an analytical compass and a justice orientation\, drawing on the man
 y intersections of his own life: family histories and personal narratives\
 , Asian masculinities\, queer continuities\, and Trinidadian/Caribbean his
 tory and culture. In examining these representations and formations he hig
 hlights policing\, immigration\, racism and homophobia within the larger c
 ontext of colonialism’s impositions. As an activist Fung was cofounder o
 f Gay Asians of Toronto (GAT) and in the arts\, cofounder of the Images Fe
 stival. He is the recipient of a Rockefeller fellowship\, the Bell Canada 
 Award for video art\, the Toronto Arts Award for media arts\, and the Kess
 ler Award from CLAGS: Center for LGBTQ Studies at the City University of N
 ew York\, among other recognitions. Richard is Professor Emeritus in the F
 aculty of Art.\n\nSea in the Blood Islands\n\nMy Mothers Funny Enigma Dog
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street Auditorium\, MCA190
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/art-creates-change-richard
 -fung
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
